5</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9247329</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Bhat2"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bhat</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Schedl</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>Requirement for <italic>engrailed</italic> and <italic>invected</italic> genes reveal novel regulatory interactions between <italic>engrailed/invected</italic>, <italic>patched</italic>, <italic>gooseberry</italic> and <italic>wingless</italic> during <italic>Drosophila</italic> neurogeneis.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>124</volume><fpage>1675</fpage><lpage>1688</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9165116</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Skeath1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Skeath</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Thor</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Genetic control of Drosophila nerve cord development.</article-title><source>Curr Opin Neurobiol</source><volume>13</volume><fpage>8</fpage><lpage>15</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12593977</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Isshiki1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Isshiki</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Pearson</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Holbrook</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Doe</surname><given-names>CQ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Drosophila neuroblasts sequentially express transcription factors which specify the temporal identity of their neuronal progeny.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>106</volume><fpage>511</fpage><lpage>521</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11525736</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Joly1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Joly</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Mugat</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Maschat</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Engrailed controls the organization of the ventral nerve cord through frazzled regulation.</article-title><source>Dev Biol</source><volume>301</volume><fpage>542</fpage><lpage>554</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17126316</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-TessierLavigne1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tessier-Lavigne</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Goodman</surname><given-names>CS</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>The molecular biology of axon guidance.</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>274</volume><fpage>1123</fpage><lpage>1133</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8895455</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Garbe1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Garbe</surname><given-names>DS</given-names></name><name><surname>Bashaw</surname><given-names>GJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Independent functions of Slit-Robo repulsion and Netrin-Frazzled attraction regulate axon crossing at the midline in Drosophila.</article-title><source>J Neurosci</source><volume>27</volume><fpage>3584</fpage><lpage>3592</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17392474</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Bate1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bate</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name></person-group><year>1976</year><article-title>Pioneer neurones in an insect embryo.</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>260</volume><fpage>54</fpage><lpage>56</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1264194</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Deshpande1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Deshpande</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Dittrich</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Technau</surname><given-names>GM</given-names></name><name><surname>Urban</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Successive specification of Drosophila neuroblasts NB 6-4 and NB 7-3 depends on interaction of the segment polarity genes wingless, gooseberry and naked cuticle.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>128</volume><fpage>3253</fpage><lpage>3261</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11546742</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Gutjahr1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gutjahr</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Patel</surname><given-names>NH</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Goodman</surname><given-names>CS</given-names></name><name><surname>Noll</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>1993</year><article-title>Analysis of the gooseberry locus in Drosophila embryos: gooseberry determines the cuticular pattern and activates gooseberry neuro.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>118</volume><fpage>21</fpage><lpage>31</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8375335</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Serrano1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Serrano</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Brock</surname><given-names>HW</given-names></name><name><surname>Demeret</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Dura</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Randsholt</surname><given-names>NB</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title><italic>polyhomeotic</italic> appears to be a target of Engrailed regulation in <italic>Drosophila</italic>.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>121</volume><fpage>1691</fpage><lpage>1703</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7600986</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Serrano2"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Serrano</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Brock</surname><given-names>HW</given-names></name><name><surname>Maschat</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title><italic>&#x000df;3-tubulin</italic> is directly repressed by the Engrailed protein in <italic>Drosophila</italic>.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>124</volume><fpage>2527</fpage><lpage>2536</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9216995</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Chanas1"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chanas</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Lavrov</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Iral</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Cavalli</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Maschat</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Engrailed and polyhomeotic maintain posterior cell identity through cubitus-interruptus regulation.</article-title><source>Dev Biol</source><volume>272</volume><fpage>522</fpage><lpage>535</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15282166</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002197-Solano1"><label>21</label><citatio